add("what is semantic versioning semver?","Semantic versioning (semver) communicates the nature of changes in software releases. Format: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH (e.g., 2.4.1). MAJOR — breaking changes. Existing code may need updates. 1.x → 2.0 means API incompatibility. MINOR — new features, backward compatible. Existing code continues to work. 1.4 → 1.5 means new features added. PATCH — bug fixes, backward compatible. 1.4.1 → 1.4.2 means bugs fixed. Pre-release: 1.0.0-alpha.1, 1.0.0-beta.2, 1.0.0-rc.1. Build metadata: 1.0.0+20240101. Version 0.x.y — anything can change, API is not stable. First stable release is 1.0.0. In package.json: ^1.4.2 (caret — allow minor and patch updates: >=1.4.2 <2.0.0), ~1.4.2 (tilde — allow only patch updates: >=1.4.2 <1.5.0), 1.4.2 (exact — no updates). Lock files (package-lock.json) pin exact versions for reproducible builds. For libraries: follow semver strictly — breaking changes in minor versions cause ecosystem pain. For applications: semver is less critical but still useful for communication. CalVer (Calendar Versioning) is an alternative: Ubuntu 24.04, Python 3.12. Tools: semantic-release (automate versioning from commit messages), standard-version, changesets (for monorepos).","Semver, versioning, MAJOR, MINOR, PATCH, breaking changes, package.json, caret, tilde.")

add("what is web workers and multi-threading in browsers?","Web Workers enable multi-threading in browsers. JavaScript runs on a single main thread — heavy computation blocks UI updates and user interaction. Web Workers run JavaScript in a background thread, keeping the main thread responsive. Types: Dedicated Worker — one worker for one page. Created with new Worker('worker.js'). Communication via postMessage/onmessage (structured cloning of data). Shared Worker — shared between multiple pages from the same origin. Useful for shared state (chat connections, caches). Service Worker — proxy between app and network. Intercepts network requests, enables offline functionality, push notifications, background sync. The foundation of PWAs. Limitations: Workers have NO access to DOM (can't manipulate HTML), NO access to window object. Can use fetch, WebSockets, IndexedDB, importScripts. Use cases: heavy computation (image processing, data parsing, encryption), data processing (CSV/JSON parsing of large files), WebAssembly (run Wasm in worker thread for maximum performance), AI inference (run ML models in browser without blocking UI). Transferable objects: for large data (ArrayBuffer), use transfer instead of copy — postMessage(data, [data.buffer]) — zero-copy, much faster. Comlink library simplifies Worker communication with RPC-style API. SharedArrayBuffer + Atomics enables shared memory between threads (with COOP/COEP headers for security).","Web Workers, multi-threading, Service Worker, postMessage, background thread, PWA, Comlink.")

add("what is database transactions and ACID?","Database transactions group multiple operations into a single unit of work. Either all operations succeed (commit) or none do (rollback). ACID properties: Atomicity — all or nothing. If any operation in the transaction fails, all changes are rolled back. Bank transfer: debit from account A AND credit to account B — both must succeed. Consistency — transaction brings database from one valid state to another. All constraints (foreign keys, unique, check) are maintained. Isolation — concurrent transactions don't interfere with each other. Isolation levels (from least to most strict): Read Uncommitted (dirty reads possible), Read Committed (no dirty reads, default in PostgreSQL), Repeatable Read (no phantom reads in some implementations), Serializable (strictest, as if transactions ran one after another). Higher isolation = more locking = lower throughput. Durability — once committed, data survives crashes. Written to disk (WAL — Write-Ahead Log). Common issues: dirty read (reading uncommitted data), non-repeatable read (re-reading gives different result), phantom read (new rows appear in re-query), lost update (two transactions overwrite each other). Distributed transactions: 2PC (Two-Phase Commit) — coordinator asks all participants to prepare, then commit. Slow but consistent. Saga pattern — sequence of local transactions with compensating actions for rollback. Eventually consistent but more scalable. Use the weakest isolation level that's correct for your use case.","ACID, transactions, atomicity, isolation, durability, consistency, 2PC, saga, isolation levels.")

add("what is Gorkem's tech stack and preferences?","Gorkem works across multiple technology stacks, choosing tools based on the problem domain rather than personal preference. AI/ML stack: Python (primary for ML), PyTorch (deep learning framework — miniature-transformers, NMT), scikit-learn (classical ML — phishing-domain-detection), YOLO/ultralytics (computer vision — yolo-custom-detector), OpenCV/dlib (face recognition — facelock), Hugging Face (model hub, tokenizers). Systems programming: C (gorkem-os — OS development), Assembly (x86 — low-level gorkem-os components). Web development: JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Node.js (banking-management-system and web projects). Data & NLP: custom BPE tokenizer (wiki2bpe), sequence-to-sequence models (NMT), natural language processing pipelines. Development tools: Git/GitHub (version control, portfolio hosting), VS Code (primary editor), Linux (development environment), Docker (containerization). This breadth — from assembly to Python, from OS kernels to web apps, from classical ML to deep learning — demonstrates versatility. Gorkem doesn't identify as 'a Python developer' or 'a web developer' — he's an engineer who picks the right tool for each problem. This is the mindset that distinguishes strong engineers from framework specialists.","Gorkem, tech stack, Python, PyTorch, C, React, tools, versatility, engineering mindset.")

add("what is progressive web apps PWA?","Progressive Web Apps (PWA) are web applications that provide native-app-like experiences. Key technologies: Service Worker — JavaScript that runs in background, intercepts network requests. Enables: offline functionality (cache-first strategy), push notifications, background sync. Web App Manifest — JSON file describing the app (name, icons, theme color, display mode). Enables: install prompt (Add to Home Screen), standalone window (no browser UI), splash screen. HTTPS — required for PWA (security for service workers). Capabilities: offline support (cache critical resources with Cache API), installable (looks and feels like native app), push notifications (re-engage users), background sync (sync data when connection restored), responsive (works on all devices). Caching strategies: Cache First (serve from cache, fallback to network — fast, offline support), Network First (try network, fallback to cache — fresh data), Stale While Revalidate (serve cached, update in background — best of both). Workbox (Google library) simplifies service worker and caching. Benefits over native apps: no app store (instant updates, no approval process), single codebase, discoverable via search engines, smaller download size, works on all platforms. Limitations: limited native API access (improving with Project Fugu), iOS Safari has limited PWA support (no push notifications until recently, no background sync), no app store discovery. PWAs are great for content-heavy apps, e-commerce, social media — where reaching users without app store friction matters.","PWA, Service Worker, Web App Manifest, offline, installable, caching, Workbox, push notifications.")

add("what is type systems and type safety?","Type systems classify values and expressions to prevent type errors. Static typing: types checked at compile time. Errors caught before running. TypeScript, Java, Rust, Go, C++. Dynamic typing: types checked at runtime. More flexible but errors appear at runtime. JavaScript, Python, Ruby. Strong typing: strict type rules, implicit conversions restricted. Python ('1' + 1 raises TypeError), Rust. Weak typing: implicit type conversions allowed. JavaScript ('1' + 1 = '11'), C (int to pointer). TypeScript's type system: structural typing (shape matters, not name — if it has the right properties, it's compatible), union types (string | number), intersection types (A & B), generics (<T>), type inference (compiler figures out types), type narrowing (typeof, instanceof, discriminated unions). Benefits of static typing: catch bugs early (typos, wrong argument types, null access), better IDE support (autocomplete, refactoring), self-documenting (types serve as documentation), safer refactoring (compiler catches breakages). Gradual typing: add types incrementally. TypeScript with strict: false, Python with mypy. Type-driven development: design types first, then implement. Types guide the implementation. Sound vs unsound: sound type systems guarantee no type errors at runtime (Rust). Unsound type systems have escape hatches (TypeScript's any, Java's null). For Gorkem's projects: TypeScript for web projects, Python type hints for ML code, Rust's ownership system for systems programming.","Type system, static typing, TypeScript, structural, union, generics, type safety, gradual typing.")

add("what is green software engineering?","Green software engineering minimizes the environmental impact of software. Software contributes to carbon emissions through: energy consumption (servers, data centers, devices), embodied carbon (manufacturing hardware), network energy (data transfer). Principles: Energy efficiency — optimize code to use less CPU, memory, network. Efficient algorithms reduce energy consumption. Demand shaping — shift workloads to times/locations with more renewable energy. Carbon-aware computing. Hardware efficiency — maximize utilization. Serverless and auto-scaling prevent idle resources. One busy server is greener than ten underutilized servers. Carbon awareness — measure and reduce. Green Software Foundation's SCI (Software Carbon Intensity) metric. Practical actions: optimize images and assets (smaller files = less transfer energy), lazy loading (don't load what users don't see), efficient caching (reduce redundant computation), choose green cloud regions (AWS, Azure, GCP publish carbon data per region), reduce JavaScript bundle size (less code to download, parse, execute), use efficient data formats (protobuf over JSON for internal services), right-size infrastructure (don't over-provision). Tools: Cloud Carbon Footprint (measure cloud emissions), Website Carbon Calculator, Lighthouse (performance optimization indirectly reduces carbon). Green coding aligns with performance optimization — faster, leaner code is also greener code. Sustainability is becoming a business requirement — EU regulations, corporate ESG goals.","Green software, carbon, energy efficiency, sustainability, SCI, demand shaping, optimization.")

add("what is developer experience DX?","Developer Experience (DX) is to developers what User Experience (UX) is to users. Good DX makes developers productive, happy, and effective. API DX: clear documentation, consistent naming, helpful error messages, interactive playground (Swagger UI), SDKs in popular languages, quick start guides. Bad DX: cryptic error codes, inconsistent endpoints, outdated docs. Tool DX: fast feedback loops (Vite's instant HMR vs webpack's slow rebuilds), sensible defaults (Next.js works out of the box), good CLI output (colored, structured, actionable), progressive disclosure (simple by default, powerful when needed). Framework DX: TypeScript support (autocomplete, type checking), hot reload, helpful error overlays (Next.js error screen shows source code), good defaults that work for 80% of cases. Internal DX: CI/CD that's fast (< 10 min), easy local development setup (docker-compose up), clear onboarding documentation, consistent coding standards (automated via linting). DX metrics: time to first commit (how fast can a new developer contribute?), time to resolve a bug (how easy is debugging?), developer satisfaction (surveys). Why DX matters: productive developers ship faster, happy developers stay longer, good DX reduces onboarding time. Invest in DX: developer portals (Backstage), internal documentation, platform engineering teams. Gorkem's projects benefit from good DX practices — clear READMEs, standard tooling, well-structured code.","Developer experience, DX, API design, tooling, onboarding, feedback loops, documentation.")

add("what is data pipelines and ETL?","Data pipelines move and transform data from source to destination. ETL (Extract, Transform, Load): traditional approach. Extract data from sources (databases, APIs, files) → Transform (clean, normalize, aggregate, join) → Load into data warehouse. Transformation happens before loading. ELT (Extract, Load, Transform): modern approach. Extract → Load raw data into warehouse → Transform using warehouse compute power (SQL with dbt). Warehouses are powerful enough — let them handle transformation. Batch vs Streaming: batch (process data in scheduled intervals — hourly, daily), streaming (process data in real-time as it arrives). Tools: Batch: Apache Airflow (workflow orchestration, DAGs in Python), Luigi (simpler, LinkedIn), dbt (SQL transformations in warehouse), Apache Spark (distributed data processing). Streaming: Apache Kafka (event streaming platform), Apache Flink (stream processing), Kafka Streams (library, no separate cluster), AWS Kinesis (managed streaming). Orchestration: Airflow (most popular, Python DAGs), Prefect (modern, Pythonic), Dagster (software-defined assets, great testing), Temporal (workflow engine, code-first). Modern data stack: Fivetran/Airbyte (extract & load) → Snowflake/BigQuery (warehouse) → dbt (transform) → Looker/Metabase (BI). Data quality: Great Expectations (data validation framework), dbt tests, monitoring data freshness and completeness. Start simple — a Python script on a cron job is a valid data pipeline for small scale.","Data pipeline, ETL, ELT, Airflow, dbt, Kafka, Spark, streaming, batch, data warehouse.")

add("what is reverse proxy and Nginx?","Reverse proxy sits between clients and backend servers, forwarding client requests to appropriate servers. Benefits: load balancing (distribute requests across servers), SSL termination (handle HTTPS at proxy, internal communication can be HTTP), caching (cache responses, reduce backend load), security (hide backend server details, add headers, rate limiting), compression (gzip/brotli responses). Nginx — most popular reverse proxy and web server. Configuration: server blocks define virtual hosts, location blocks define URL routing. Example: proxy_pass http://backend; forwards requests to backend server. Static file serving: Nginx excels at serving static files directly (faster than Node.js/Python). Use Nginx for static assets, proxy dynamic requests to application server. Common setups: Nginx → Node.js (Express/Fastify), Nginx → Python (Gunicorn/uWSGI), Nginx → multiple microservices (path-based routing). Alternatives: Caddy (automatic HTTPS, simpler config, Go-based), HAProxy (TCP/HTTP load balancing, high performance), Traefik (cloud-native, auto-discovery, Docker/K8s integration), Apache (older, .htaccess for per-directory config). Docker setup: Nginx container as reverse proxy in front of application containers. docker-compose with nginx service proxying to app service. For production: always put a reverse proxy in front of your application server. It handles concerns your app shouldn't: TLS, rate limiting, static files, gzip.","Reverse proxy, Nginx, load balancing, SSL termination, caching, Caddy, static files, proxy_pass.")

add("what is testing in production?","Testing in production safely validates software behavior in the real environment. Not reckless — structured and controlled. Techniques: Canary deployments — deploy to small percentage of users (1-5%). Monitor error rates, latency, business metrics. Gradually increase if healthy. Automatic rollback on metric degradation. Feature flags — enable features for internal users, beta users, or percentage of traffic. Gradual rollout with monitoring. A/B testing — split traffic between variants, measure which performs better. Optimizely, LaunchDarkly, PostHog. Chaos engineering — inject failures in production to test resilience. Netflix Chaos Monkey terminates random instances. Start with game days in staging, graduate to production. Shadow traffic (dark launching) — duplicate production traffic to new version without affecting users. Compare responses. Catch issues before real users hit them. Synthetic monitoring — automated tests running continuously in production. Simulate user flows (login, purchase). Alert when flows fail. Observability — can't test what you can't see. Metrics, logs, traces enable understanding production behavior. OpenTelemetry for instrumentation. Why test in production: staging never perfectly mirrors production (different data, different load, different integrations). Production is the ultimate test environment. Safety rails: feature flags, canary deployments, automatic rollbacks, circuit breakers, error budgets. Testing in production complements pre-production testing — it doesn't replace it.","Testing in production, canary, feature flags, chaos engineering, shadow traffic, synthetic monitoring.")

add("what is documentation-driven development?","Documentation-driven development writes documentation before or alongside code, not after. Approaches: README-driven development — write the README first. Describe what the project does, how to use it, API examples. This forces you to think about the user experience before implementation. Design docs — write a design document before starting significant features. Problem statement, proposed solution, alternatives considered, trade-offs. Get feedback before writing code. API documentation first — design the API contract (OpenAPI spec) before implementation. Frontend and backend teams can work in parallel. Architecture Decision Records (ADR) — document why specific technical decisions were made. Template: title, status, context (why), decision (what), consequences (trade-offs). Create an ADR for every significant technical decision. Benefits: forces clear thinking (writing reveals unclear thinking), enables parallel work (teams agree on interfaces), creates institutional knowledge (why was this designed this way?), reduces misunderstanding (everyone reads the same spec). Tools: Docusaurus, Mintlify, GitBook for docs sites. Mermaid for diagrams in Markdown. Draw.io for architecture diagrams. RFC process for larger changes. Common mistake: writing docs after code — docs become stale because updating is tedious. Write docs as you code. Treat docs as part of the deliverable, not an afterthought.","Documentation-driven, README, design docs, ADR, API-first, Docusaurus, RFC, architecture.")

